What is Complexity?

When we write a program, we want to know:

How fast does it run? → Time Complexity
How much memory does it use? → Space Complexity

n = number of elements

Time Complexity

How many operations an algorithm performs as the input size increases.
It is written using Big O Notation.
O(...)

Example 1: O(1) - Constant Time
No matter how big the array is, only one operation is performed.

int[] arr = {10,20,30,40,50};
System.out.println(arr[0]);
Even if the array has
5 elements
500 elements
500000 elements
Only one element is accessed.
Time Complexity = 0(1)

Example 2: O(n)

for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
Suppose
n = 5
Prints 5 times
if
n = 100
Prints 100 times
Operations increase directly with input size.
Time Complexity = O(n)

Example 3: O(n²)
Nested loops
for(int i=0;i<n;i++){

    for(int j=0;j<n;j++){

        System.out.println(i+" "+j);

    }

}
Suppose
n = 3
Iterations
(0,0)
(0,1)
(0,2)

(1,0)
(1,1)
(1,2)

(2,0)
(2,1)
(2,2)

Total
3 × 3 = 9
jf n=100
100 ×100

10000 operations
Time Complexity = O(n²)

Example 4: O(log n)

This appears in Binary Search.
Array = 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9
Target = 8
Instead of checking every element,
We check the middle.
1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9
        ^
 8 is greater.
 Ignore left half.
 Now
 6 7 8 9
 7
 Again ignore half.
 Now
 8 9
 Found.
 Each step removes half the remaining elements.
 Time Complexity = O(log n)


 Example 5: O(n log n)
Sorting algorithms like:
Merge Sort
Heap Sort
Average-case Quick Sort
Merge Sort splits the array repeatedly and merges it back.
8 elements
Split
8
↓
4 + 4
↓
2 +2 +2 +2
↓
1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
Each level processes all n elements, and there are log n levels.
Time Complexity = O(n log n)


