Summary of Time Complexities
| Complexity | Name         | Example             |
| ---------- | ------------ | ------------------- |
| O(1)       | Constant     | Array index access  |
| O(log n)   | Logarithmic  | Binary Search       |
| O(n)       | Linear       | Linear Search       |
| O(n log n) | Linearithmic | Merge Sort          |
| O(n²)      | Quadratic    | Nested loops        |
| O(n³)      | Cubic        | Triple nested loops |
| O(2ⁿ)      | Exponential  | Recursive Fibonacci |
| O(n!)      | Factorial    | Permutations        |



Space Complexity
Space Complexity is the amount of memory (RAM) an algorithm needs while it is running.
This includes:

Variables
Arrays
Objects
Function call stack (recursion)
Any extra memory created during execution

Example 1: O(1)

int a = 10;
int b = 20;
int c = a + b;

Only three variables.
Whether input is
10
100
10000
Memory doesn't change.
Space Complexity
O(1)

Example 2: O(n)

int[] arr = new int[n];
If
n = 5
Memory = 5 integers
If
n = 1000
Memory = 1000 integers
Space grows with input.
O(n)

Example 3: Copying an Array
int[] copy = new int[n];

for(int i=0;i<n;i++){

    copy[i]=arr[i];

}
Original array already exists.
Extra memory used
copy[]
size = n
Space Complexity
O(n)

